The Human-English works in a complete lattice, with bottom the supremum of the empty set and top the supremum of the whole lattice, and defines \(x \ll y\) to mean that \(y\) lies in the interior, for the induced topology, of the principal upset of \(x\); Proposition 2.2 then asserts (i) \(\bot \ll x\), (ii) \(x \ll z\) and \(y \ll z\) imply \(x \sqcup y \ll z\), (iii) \(x \ll y \sqsubseteq z\) implies \(x \ll z\), (iv) \(x \sqsubseteq y \ll z\) implies \(x \ll z\), (v) \(x \ll y\) implies \(x \sqsubseteq y\), (vi) \(x \ll x\) if and only if the principal upset of \(x\) is open, and (vii) for directed \(S\), \(x \ll \bigsqcup S\) if and only if \(x \ll y\) for some \(y \in S\). After the proposition it records a second characterization: \(x \ll y\) if and only if whenever \(y \sqsubseteq \bigsqcup Z\) for a directed \(Z\), some \(z \in Z\) satisfies \(x \sqsubseteq z\). `WayBelow` is the interior relation, witnessed by a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) contained in the principal upset of \(x\), and `notation5` writes it \(\ll\). `bot_wayBelow` proves (i) because the universe is a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(x\) and is contained in the upset of bottom. `WayBelowu2esup` proves (ii) by intersecting the two witnessing neighbourhoods, so a point of the intersection lies above both \(x\) and \(y\) and hence above their join. `WayBelowu2etrans_le` proves (iii) because a Scott-open set is an upper set, so the neighbourhood of \(y\) already contains \(z\). `WayBelowu2ele_trans` proves (iv) because a neighbourhood contained in the upset of \(y\) is contained in the upset of \(x\). `WayBelowu2ele` proves (v) because \(y\) itself lies in the witnessing neighbourhood. `wayBelow_self_iff_scottOpen_Ici` proves (vi): if \(x \ll x\), a witnessing neighbourhood contained in the principal upset equals that upset, since the neighbourhood is an upper set containing \(x\), and conversely a Scott-open principal upset is a neighbourhood of \(x\) contained in itself. `wayBelow_sSup_iff` proves (vii) for a nonempty directed set, matching the paper’s convention that directed sets are nonempty: a witnessing neighbourhood of the supremum meets the set, and the same neighbourhood witnesses \(x \ll s\) at the meeting point, while the converse is monotonicity on the right because the member lies below the supremum. `scottOpen_wayBelow` adds that the set of elements way-above a fixed element is Scott-open, which is not one of the seven clauses. The later biconditional, with \(\sqsubseteq\) rather than \(\ll\) on the right and with an arbitrary directed set bounded above by \(y\), is not the statement of `wayBelow_sSup_iff`. **Theorem:** partial — clauses (i)–(vii) and the interior definition are formalized, and the directed-supremum characterization of \(\ll\) is not. **Proof:** yes — each clause is derived from an interior witness by the intersection, upper-set transport, or directed-meeting argument that clause requires.
